{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"44dbc4dde4cdf9599d86be54d536547be4307cb17433b3a9d4ddb4f87a4e3e7c","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"assert_community_optical_tradeoffs","metadata":{"topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"assertion","object":"Participants describe fewer optical switch options and possible debounce-related latency benefits while explicitly lacking evidence that the difference is noticeable.","predicate":"report","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"postmill","source_url":"http://localhost:9999/f/MechanicalKeyboards/14352/mechanical-vs-optical-switches","subject":"mechanical-versus-optical thread participants","verifier":{"kind":"quoted_assertion"}}
{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"44dbc4dde4cdf9599d86be54d536547be4307cb17433b3a9d4ddb4f87a4e3e7c","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"prop_community_optical_scope","metadata":{"acceptable_source_roles":["community"],"critical":true,"scope":"multi_commenter_non_instrumented_discussion","topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"proposition","object":"Thread participants describe a smaller optical-switch ecosystem, possible response or debounce-related latency advantages, and explicit uncertainty about whether the latency difference is noticeable. These are scoped opinions, not instrumented SK61-versus-K6 results.","predicate":"reports_tradeoffs_and_uncertainty","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"postmill","source_url":"http://localhost:9999/f/MechanicalKeyboards/14352/mechanical-vs-optical-switches","subject":"mechanical-versus-optical community discussion","verifier":{"accepted_phrases":["Community participants describe optical-switch choice limits and possible latency advantages while explicitly saying they have not seen evidence that the difference is noticeable; the thread is not an instrumented comparison of the SK61 and K6."],"kind":"typed_claim","matcher":"normalized_text","normalizers":["casefold","whitespace","punctuation","hyphen"]}}
{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"5429ba4ea034c00765f8d79cb63068b04d8ff2d0dabcc192667096e997849881","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"assert_nkro_definition","metadata":{"topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"assertion","object":"NKRO independently scans keys so simultaneous held keys can still be detected, a property relevant to multi-key gaming inputs.","predicate":"states","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"wikipedia","source_url":"http://localhost:8090/content/wikipedia_en_all_nopic/Key_rollover","subject":"Key rollover article","verifier":{"kind":"quoted_assertion"}}
{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"5429ba4ea034c00765f8d79cb63068b04d8ff2d0dabcc192667096e997849881","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"prop_nkro_scope","metadata":{"acceptable_source_roles":["concept"],"critical":true,"topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"proposition","object":"NKRO means each key can be detected independently while other keys are held, preventing missed or ghosted multi-key combinations. This is physically useful for some gaming inputs, but the definition does not establish faster single-key actuation, lower end-to-end latency, or improved aim.","predicate":"handles_simultaneous_keys_not_single_key_latency","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"wikipedia","source_url":"http://localhost:8090/content/wikipedia_en_all_nopic/Key_rollover","subject":"N-key rollover","verifier":{"accepted_phrases":["NKRO independently detects simultaneous keys and addresses multi-key loss, ghosting, and jamming; that rollover property is not itself evidence of lower single-key latency or better aim."],"kind":"typed_claim","matcher":"normalized_text","normalizers":["casefold","whitespace","punctuation","hyphen"]}}
{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"5b7f20e3df50e7279b638687a02c665856cc17c9e77f5cec1e804b3039782c05","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"bridge_keyboard_feature_matrix","metadata":{"rule_id":"keyboard_feature_matrix_v1","topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"bridge","object":"Both listings are below $100 and claim wired NKRO. The K6 is $83.99 with 68 keys, dedicated arrows, wired NKRO and 6KRO wireless; the SK61 is $94.39 with 61 keys, NKRO wording, and optical-only hot swapping. The SK61 title and specifications disagree on optical switch color, and neither body states a polling rate or measured latency.","predicate":"derives","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"case_spec","source_url":"http://case-spec.local/dra_v3_dev_keyboard_0005","subject":"two-board frozen feature matrix","verifier":{"kind":"deterministic_rule"}}
{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"5b7f20e3df50e7279b638687a02c665856cc17c9e77f5cec1e804b3039782c05","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"bridge_optical_debounce_scope","metadata":{"rule_id":"optical_debounce_scope_v1","topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"bridge","object":"Optical sensing and contact debouncing are real mechanisms. The SK61 implements optical switches, while community participants describe both possible response advantages and a restricted switch ecosystem, yet explicitly lack evidence that the latency difference is noticeable. No captured source provides an instrumented SK61-versus-K6 latency or aim test.","predicate":"reconciles","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"case_spec","source_url":"http://case-spec.local/dra_v3_dev_keyboard_0005","subject":"optical mechanism and owner-claim scope","verifier":{"kind":"deterministic_rule"}}
{"body_support":true,"content_sha256":"5b7f20e3df50e7279b638687a02c665856cc17c9e77f5cec1e804b3039782c05","corpus_snapshot":"dra-v3-pilot-keyboard-latency-my5090-20260716-r2","evidence_id":"bridge_polling_interval_boundary","metadata":{"rule_id":"polling_interval_boundary_v1","topic_cluster":"keyboard_latency_claims"},"node_type":"bridge","object":"USB HID polling is a real report path and hertz permits a hypothetical 0.125 ms period at 8000 Hz versus 1 ms at 1000 Hz. Because neither product body states an actual polling rate and no source measures the rest of the input-to-display path or human perception, the arithmetic cannot justify an 8000 Hz premium here.","predicate":"derives","search_snippet_support":false,"source_type":"case_spec","source_url":"http://case-spec.local/dra_v3_dev_keyboard_0005","subject":"8000 Hz polling claim boundary","verifier":{"kind":"deterministic_rule"}}
